1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,650
Mom! Come in.

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,960
Welcome, Ruth. We kept the afternoon free.

3
00:00:05,110 --> 00:00:09,590
Then I have brought good company and a small
surprise.

4
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:14,220
Come sit by us. The surprise can wait a
minute.

5
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:18,850
Six packing cubes, one for each of you.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,160
My socks are receiving their own apartments.

7
00:00:22,310 --> 00:00:24,150
They need addresses first.

8
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:28,780
Then you children can help the apartments find
their owners.

9
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:34,614
Once, Dan packed a tiny sock instead of his
necktie.

10
00:00:34,764 --> 00:00:36,986
A very compact fashion choice.

11
00:00:37,136 --> 00:00:39,357
Did it match your shirt?

12
00:00:39,507 --> 00:00:44,300
I never got far enough to try it under my
collar.

13
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:48,410
Your suitcase can go on this stand.

14
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:51,720
The top drawer is empty for you.

15
00:00:51,870 --> 00:00:54,150
You have thought of everything.

16
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:59,220
Good. There's room for you to unpack at your
own pace.

17
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:03,146
This checklist keeps my own things together.

18
00:01:03,296 --> 00:01:04,982
An excellent traveling companion.

19
00:01:05,132 --> 00:01:09,238
We will leave your lavender pouch just as it
is.

20
00:01:09,388 --> 00:01:14,300
Thank you. I like finding that one just where
I put it.

21
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:19,237
We noticed before he left. He borrowed a
proper tie.

22
00:01:19,387 --> 00:01:22,592
So the sock did not attend the meeting?

23
00:01:22,742 --> 00:01:24,379
It had other appointments.

24
00:01:24,529 --> 00:01:29,300
I hope it found a shoe with a less formal
dress code.

25
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:35,728
Use the cubes and borrow my checklist idea. I
will rest next door.

26
00:01:35,878 --> 00:01:37,200
Of course, Mom.

27
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:39,919
We will keep your room peaceful.

28
00:01:40,069 --> 00:01:44,300
Thanks, girls. I'll look forward to hearing
how it went.

29
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:47,900
Thank you for the cubes, Grandma.

30
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:52,414
You are welcome. Tell me how the new
apartments work.

31
00:01:52,564 --> 00:01:54,786
We will, after your rest.

32
00:01:54,936 --> 00:01:59,300
I already have a pair of tenants in mind,
Grandma.

33
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:02,970
These socks are clean and dry.

34
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:06,280
And these cubes still have no names.

35
00:02:06,430 --> 00:02:09,150
Let us sort before we store.

36
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:13,780
Good. A gray cube doesn't tell you much by
itself.

37
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:17,970
I can see my green heels.

38
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:20,400
A whole family of socks.

39
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:24,150
Six pairs. We will check the owners together.

40
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:29,220
I'll know those green bits even with my eyes
half shut.

41
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:34,290
Six pairs and six places. I can manage that.

42
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:38,480
One pair in each cube. What could go wrong?

43
00:02:38,630 --> 00:02:40,910
Green heels in this one.

44
00:02:41,060 --> 00:02:44,220
There. Its neighbor gets the next pair.

45
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:48,329
That zipper went all the way around.

46
00:02:48,479 --> 00:02:52,414
They are packed. The names can catch up later.

47
00:02:52,564 --> 00:02:55,214
Which one had the green heels?

48
00:02:55,364 --> 00:02:59,300
Ah, they're all snug. I'll sort out names
later.

49
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:02,530
Now where should these go?

50
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:06,720
Packing cubes, suitcase. That seems a very
strong connection.

51
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:09,150
Well, look who brought them.

52
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:13,340
Grandma's case has exactly the right sort of
space.

53
00:03:15,250 --> 00:03:17,805
There's room on this side.

54
00:03:17,955 --> 00:03:21,003
Six small apartments, one large building.

55
00:03:21,153 --> 00:03:23,216
That's all six together.

56
00:03:23,366 --> 00:03:27,400
The lavender pouch can have its own side.

57
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:32,530
There. The case closes nicely.

58
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:35,840
Everything put away. A remarkably tidy
afternoon.

59
00:03:35,990 --> 00:03:38,710
Funny how quiet the counter looks.

60
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:42,460
Wait till everybody sees how quickly I
finished.

61
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:47,189
Where are the socks?

62
00:03:47,339 --> 00:03:48,351
Safely packed.

63
00:03:48,501 --> 00:03:49,976
For which destination?

64
00:03:50,126 --> 00:03:54,384
Well, that depends on whose suitcase we're
talking about.

65
00:03:54,534 --> 00:03:57,400
Dan, whose suitcase did you use?

66
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:03,850
My socks are not in their new apartment.

67
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:05,840
Perhaps the building moved.

68
00:04:05,990 --> 00:04:08,270
I may have arranged transportation.

69
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:13,340
Did you tell my feet? They didn't pack for a
trip.

70
00:04:15,250 --> 00:04:17,530
The labels never went anywhere.

71
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:21,280
Then let us ask the person who packed.

72
00:04:21,430 --> 00:04:23,270
That would be me.

73
00:04:23,420 --> 00:04:27,900
Which way did you go after you closed the
zippers?

74
00:04:30,250 --> 00:04:35,565
I put our six cubes in Ruth's suitcase. I
assumed they were hers.

75
00:04:35,715 --> 00:04:37,805
Thank you for telling us.

76
00:04:37,955 --> 00:04:40,448
Then we know where to look.

77
00:04:40,598 --> 00:04:44,300
Good thing she hasn't taken that suitcase
anywhere yet.

78
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:49,730
We can retrieve our gifts and leave Grandma's
pouch alone.

79
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:52,600
Then describe, ask, label, and store.

80
00:04:52,750 --> 00:04:55,030
Four steps. No moving buildings.

81
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:59,220
That's right. We'll only move what belongs to
us.

82
00:05:00,250 --> 00:05:03,410
This is exactly where I left them.

83
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:05,840
Open the main compartment gently.

84
00:05:05,990 --> 00:05:08,270
The lavender pouch stays closed.

85
00:05:08,420 --> 00:05:12,460
Got it. I can see the two areas now.

86
00:05:15,250 --> 00:05:19,614
Gray cubes on the left. Lavender pouch on the
right.

87
00:05:19,764 --> 00:05:22,843
An address I should have checked earlier.

88
00:05:22,993 --> 00:05:25,643
Which side are we reaching into?

89
00:05:25,793 --> 00:05:29,300
Just the gray side. Her pouch stays put.

90
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:33,061
One, two, three, four, five, six.

91
00:05:33,211 --> 00:05:34,658
Six gifts recovered.

92
00:05:34,808 --> 00:05:37,620
And nobody needed a boarding pass.

93
00:05:37,770 --> 00:05:42,400
And all six came back on the very same trip.

94
00:05:45,250 --> 00:05:48,175
My new travel-size socks. Very economical.

95
00:05:48,325 --> 00:05:50,304
Those are mine, Dad.

96
00:05:50,454 --> 00:05:53,379
Let us check, rather than guess.

97
00:05:53,529 --> 00:05:57,400
Okay, Max. What gives this particular pair
away?

98
00:06:00,250 --> 00:06:04,186
Mine have green heels, and both socks fit me.

99
00:06:04,336 --> 00:06:06,986
Excellent. My feet were being ambitious.

100
00:06:07,136 --> 00:06:09,786
Back to the clean sorting counter.

101
00:06:09,936 --> 00:06:14,300
Good. I'd rather my socks stayed the size they
are.

102
00:06:15,250 --> 00:06:18,549
Do we need to sort Grandma's pouch too?

103
00:06:18,699 --> 00:06:21,192
No. Those are her private things.

104
00:06:21,342 --> 00:06:24,641
We have only our own cubes to fix.

105
00:06:24,791 --> 00:06:29,300
So we can help her best by leaving that one
closed.

106
00:06:30,250 --> 00:06:34,730
Describe the pair, ask its owner, then add a
name.

107
00:06:34,880 --> 00:06:37,160
Before I invent a destination.

108
00:06:37,310 --> 00:06:39,150
That order sounds promising.

109
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:43,340
Exactly. No cube goes anywhere till its owner
agrees.

110
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:49,730
We can borrow the method without copying
Grandma's personal list.

111
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:51,720
Six names, six pairs.

112
00:06:51,870 --> 00:06:54,150
And we check each one.

113
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:58,780
Good. We'll write down what belongs to our own
family.

114
00:07:00,250 --> 00:07:02,090
No suitcase this time.

115
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:04,960
Good. My socks prefer staying home.

116
00:07:05,110 --> 00:07:07,830
Let us start at this end.

117
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:12,460
I'll look at the heels before we guess about
sizes.

118
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:18,850
There, that's the last thing back in place.

119
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:23,480
Your own things are right where you left them,
Mom.

120
00:07:23,630 --> 00:07:25,910
I'll leave the pouch closed.

121
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:29,220
Sleep well. The sorting can wait outside.

122
00:07:30,250 --> 00:07:32,530
The room is tidy again.

123
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:35,400
Now we finish our own sorting.

124
00:07:35,550 --> 00:07:37,830
One owner at a time.

125
00:07:37,980 --> 00:07:42,460
Good. I want to know whose pair we're holding
first.

126
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:47,805
What makes this pair yours?

127
00:07:47,955 --> 00:07:50,510
Green heels and my size.

128
00:07:50,660 --> 00:07:52,229
Evidence beats enthusiasm.

129
00:07:52,379 --> 00:07:57,400
Look, both heels are green. I checked the
other one.

130
00:08:00,250 --> 00:08:01,650
Two matching socks.

131
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:02,760
Both mine.

132
00:08:02,910 --> 00:08:05,190
Then the owner is settled.

133
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:09,820
Good. The pair has a name before the cube
does.

134
00:08:09,970 --> 00:08:14,010
Good. I don't want to open six every morning.

135
00:08:15,250 --> 00:08:18,757
Name on the cube, not on the sock.

136
00:08:18,907 --> 00:08:20,700
My toes appreciate that.

137
00:08:20,850 --> 00:08:24,357
And it can be read without opening it.

138
00:08:24,507 --> 00:08:29,300
Then I can find it even before school in the
morning.

139
00:08:30,250 --> 00:08:31,819
Mine, checked, closed.

140
00:08:31,969 --> 00:08:35,017
The first apartment has an owner.

141
00:08:35,167 --> 00:08:38,709
Can you read my name from there?

142
00:08:38,859 --> 00:08:42,400
Sure can. No need to unzip anything.

143
00:08:45,250 --> 00:08:47,090
These have coral stars.

144
00:08:47,240 --> 00:08:49,520
A famous constellation of toes.

145
00:08:49,670 --> 00:08:51,070
Who recognizes them?

146
00:08:51,220 --> 00:08:55,260
I do. I picked that pattern for a reason.

147
00:08:55,410 --> 00:08:59,010
Then I'd better put your name on them.

148
00:09:00,250 --> 00:09:03,410
They are mine. I chose the stars.

149
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:05,400
Yes, and both match.

150
00:09:05,550 --> 00:09:08,270
My green ones are already home.

151
00:09:08,420 --> 00:09:12,900
And my stars can have their own place next
door.

152
00:09:15,250 --> 00:09:17,090
Ella, on the handle.

153
00:09:17,240 --> 00:09:18,640
Not Grandma's suitcase.

154
00:09:18,790 --> 00:09:20,190
Correct destination, too.

155
00:09:20,340 --> 00:09:24,820
Good. I want to find them without taking a
trip.

156
00:09:24,970 --> 00:09:28,130
We can leave the suitcase to Grandma.

157
00:09:30,250 --> 00:09:32,702
Orange heels, longer than Max's.

158
00:09:32,852 --> 00:09:34,357
Those are mine.

159
00:09:34,507 --> 00:09:37,432
Your feet have been busy growing.

160
00:09:37,582 --> 00:09:42,400
That's how I can spot them beside his little
pair.

161
00:09:45,250 --> 00:09:47,970
May I label this cube Leo?

162
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:50,400
Yes. That is my pair.

163
00:09:50,550 --> 00:09:53,710
A short question saves a long search.

164
00:09:53,860 --> 00:09:57,900
Good. Now I can find them without opening
Max's.

165
00:10:00,250 --> 00:10:01,650
Checked and zipped.

166
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,200
Three owners found.

167
00:10:03,350 --> 00:10:05,190
And no departures scheduled.

168
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:09,820
Three more at home, then. We're halfway down
the row.

169
00:10:09,970 --> 00:10:13,130
I can see my name from here.

170
00:10:15,250 --> 00:10:17,607
Plum dots. These are mine.

171
00:10:17,757 --> 00:10:19,658
I nearly said Nina.

172
00:10:19,808 --> 00:10:23,074
Nearly is a good time to ask.

173
00:10:23,224 --> 00:10:27,400
Maya, are the plum dots the ones you picked?

174
00:10:30,250 --> 00:10:32,090
I chose the dots.

175
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:34,080
Mine are plain teal.

176
00:10:34,230 --> 00:10:37,830
Thank you. Two people, two answers worth
hearing.

177
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:43,340
We can see why guessing would've put them in
the wrong cube.

178
00:10:45,250 --> 00:10:47,070
Maya. Plum dots. Confirmed.

179
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:49,039
And safely stored together.

180
00:10:49,189 --> 00:10:51,444
And whose name faces out?

181
00:10:51,594 --> 00:10:55,155
Mine. I can read it from right here.

182
00:10:55,305 --> 00:10:59,300
Good. That's one search I won't have to make.

183
00:11:00,250 --> 00:11:02,970
These are the plain teal ones.

184
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:05,400
Yes, that is my pair.

185
00:11:05,550 --> 00:11:08,270
The description arrived before the guess.

186
00:11:08,420 --> 00:11:12,460
Good. That saves somebody opening the wrong
cube tomorrow.

187
00:11:15,250 --> 00:11:17,970
Would six identical pairs be easier?

188
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:20,840
Not for telling whose is whose.

189
00:11:20,990 --> 00:11:22,830
Different can be useful.

190
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:27,460
Good thing my heels are green. They're quicker
to spot.

191
00:11:30,250 --> 00:11:31,210
Nina, checked.

192
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:32,320
Thank you.

193
00:11:32,470 --> 00:11:35,630
Five pairs, five names. One to go.

194
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:40,260
Good. I think the last one's pretty easy to
recognize.

195
00:11:40,410 --> 00:11:43,570
Let's still ask the person they fit.

196
00:11:45,250 --> 00:11:47,090
Navy stripes. Largest size.

197
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:49,080
Those are definitely mine.

198
00:11:49,230 --> 00:11:50,630
Much less travel-sized.

199
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:55,260
Thank goodness. I couldn't get a toe into
Max's pair.

200
00:11:55,410 --> 00:11:58,570
That would've been quite a squeeze, Dad.

201
00:12:00,250 --> 00:12:02,970
Suitable for feet. Unsuitable for meetings.

202
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:05,400
We will tell your collar.

203
00:12:05,550 --> 00:12:07,390
No sock tie today.

204
00:12:07,540 --> 00:12:12,460
Good. My neck and my feet each get the right
outfit.

205
00:12:15,250 --> 00:12:17,970
Dan. Navy stripes. Staying at home.

206
00:12:18,120 --> 00:12:20,400
That is a complete address.

207
00:12:20,550 --> 00:12:22,390
Six out of six.

208
00:12:22,540 --> 00:12:27,460
Great. Now let's give all six an address we
can find.

209
00:12:30,250 --> 00:12:32,530
Six names, with no duplicates.

210
00:12:32,680 --> 00:12:34,960
Now choose the storage places.

211
00:12:35,110 --> 00:12:37,830
Then we can find them again.

212
00:12:37,980 --> 00:12:42,460
Somewhere that makes sense when we're looking
in a hurry.

213
00:12:45,250 --> 00:12:47,805
Six pairs means twelve socks.

214
00:12:47,955 --> 00:12:49,031
Twelve feet?

215
00:12:49,181 --> 00:12:52,229
Six people, two feet each. Fortunately.

216
00:12:52,379 --> 00:12:57,400
Good. None of those twelve has to travel by
itself.

217
00:13:00,250 --> 00:13:02,530
No lonely socks left here.

218
00:13:02,680 --> 00:13:04,520
All twelve accounted for.

219
00:13:04,670 --> 00:13:07,390
Then the repair really is finished.

220
00:13:07,540 --> 00:13:12,900
Good. The basket bottom was the last place I
wanted to check.

221
00:13:15,250 --> 00:13:17,970
Should we count one more time?

222
00:13:18,120 --> 00:13:20,400
We checked each pair already.

223
00:13:20,550 --> 00:13:24,590
Now we keep the record and move them once.

224
00:13:24,740 --> 00:13:29,220
Okay. I'd rather find mine than count them all
again.

225
00:13:30,250 --> 00:13:32,970
Adults across the top, children below.

226
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:35,400
Everyone can reach without climbing.

227
00:13:35,550 --> 00:13:38,710
A building with an excellent floor plan.

228
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:43,780
Then keep the names facing this way as they go
in.

229
00:13:45,250 --> 00:13:48,410
From where we stand: Dan, Maya, Nina.

230
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:50,840
And Leo, Ella, Max underneath.

231
00:13:50,990 --> 00:13:52,830
Same viewpoint. Clear directions.

232
00:13:52,980 --> 00:13:57,900
Good. It matters which way you're standing
when you say left.

233
00:14:00,250 --> 00:14:02,530
Mine is in the middle.

234
00:14:02,680 --> 00:14:04,960
Mine is on the right.

235
00:14:05,110 --> 00:14:08,270
And I finally know where mine lives.

236
00:14:08,420 --> 00:14:12,900
And all three labels face the person doing the
looking.

237
00:14:15,250 --> 00:14:16,100
Left.

238
00:14:16,250 --> 00:14:17,100
Middle.

239
00:14:17,250 --> 00:14:19,530
Right. My apartment is downstairs.

240
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:24,600
Good. Face the shelf and you can point to your
own.

241
00:14:24,750 --> 00:14:28,350
I can reach the handle all by myself.

242
00:14:30,250 --> 00:14:33,410
We can read them before opening anything.

243
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:35,840
That will help tomorrow too.

244
00:14:35,990 --> 00:14:39,150
A useful label explains, then stays put.

245
00:14:39,300 --> 00:14:44,220
Tomorrow I'll only have to open the one with
my name.

246
00:14:45,250 --> 00:14:48,298
Even the marker has a place.

247
00:14:48,448 --> 00:14:51,003
Does it need a suitcase?

248
00:14:51,153 --> 00:14:52,722
Only its cap.

249
00:14:52,872 --> 00:14:57,400
Good. It wouldn't have much to wear on
vacation.

250
00:15:00,250 --> 00:15:03,850
This can wait for the next clean load.

251
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,720
Empty, correctly located, and not traveling.

252
00:15:06,870 --> 00:15:10,470
Shall we leave it here by the washer?

253
00:15:10,620 --> 00:15:13,780
Yes. That'll make the next load easier.

254
00:15:15,250 --> 00:15:16,650
Mine is bottom-right.

255
00:15:16,800 --> 00:15:18,640
Mine is beside yours.

256
00:15:18,790 --> 00:15:22,830
And mine is on the left. No search needed.

257
00:15:22,980 --> 00:15:27,460
I like directions I can use before I'm fully
awake.

258
00:15:30,250 --> 00:15:32,403
A clean towel for Mom.

259
00:15:32,553 --> 00:15:35,538
She said the top drawer was free.

260
00:15:35,688 --> 00:15:39,503
Let us keep the rest just as it is.

261
00:15:39,653 --> 00:15:44,300
Right. She gets to choose what goes in her own
things.

262
00:15:45,250 --> 00:15:47,970
The towel goes in this drawer?

263
00:15:48,120 --> 00:15:50,840
Yes. That is the guest drawer.

264
00:15:50,990 --> 00:15:53,270
You checked the destination first.

265
00:15:53,420 --> 00:15:57,900
I like asking before I have to carry it back.

266
00:16:00,250 --> 00:16:02,013
One towel, top drawer.

267
00:16:02,163 --> 00:16:03,925
A very uneventful delivery.

268
00:16:04,075 --> 00:16:05,838
Sometimes that is perfect.

269
00:16:05,988 --> 00:16:10,280
Especially if you're trying to give somebody a
quiet afternoon.

270
00:16:10,430 --> 00:16:14,300
Let's leave the rest just how Mom likes it.

271
00:16:15,250 --> 00:16:18,410
The blanket was already where it belongs.

272
00:16:18,560 --> 00:16:21,720
I can admire that without packing it.

273
00:16:21,870 --> 00:16:22,830
Excellent restraint.

274
00:16:22,980 --> 00:16:27,460
Thanks. A folded blanket doesn't really need a
new address.

275
00:16:30,250 --> 00:16:33,850
We did not find any pajamas to hang.

276
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,280
They are Grandma's to unpack.

277
00:16:36,430 --> 00:16:39,150
Helping includes leaving her a choice.

278
00:16:39,300 --> 00:16:44,220
So she can hang them herself if she feels like
it.

279
00:16:45,250 --> 00:16:47,530
Grandma's list goes back here.

280
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:50,400
Our own record comes with us.

281
00:16:50,550 --> 00:16:52,830
Two lists, two clear owners.

282
00:16:52,980 --> 00:16:57,460
Good. Grandma won't have to hunt for her own
list.

283
00:17:00,250 --> 00:17:02,090
Hands washed after sorting.

284
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:04,960
Even though the socks were clean?

285
00:17:05,110 --> 00:17:08,710
Yes. We are finished with the laundry now.

286
00:17:08,860 --> 00:17:12,900
Good. My hands can retire from sock duty now.

287
00:17:15,250 --> 00:17:17,970
Your questions made the sorting easy.

288
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:21,280
And your shelf plan made it useful.

289
00:17:21,430 --> 00:17:23,710
I benefited from both departments.

290
00:17:23,860 --> 00:17:28,780
So tomorrow morning nobody has to ask where
their socks went.

291
00:17:30,250 --> 00:17:35,043
I wanted to make the visit easy. I moved too
fast.

292
00:17:35,193 --> 00:17:37,414
You fixed it with us.

293
00:17:37,564 --> 00:17:40,214
Next time, ask before the suitcase.

294
00:17:40,364 --> 00:17:44,300
Agreed. Even a quick question would've saved
two trips.

295
00:17:45,250 --> 00:17:47,970
Our socks almost went on vacation.

296
00:17:48,120 --> 00:17:50,840
A journey of approximately one hallway.

297
00:17:50,990 --> 00:17:54,150
And a return ticket paid in questions.

298
00:17:54,300 --> 00:17:58,340
Next time they're staying here without buying
a ticket.

299
00:18:00,250 --> 00:18:02,530
A peaceful table at last.

300
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:04,960
With everyone's feet properly represented.

301
00:18:05,110 --> 00:18:07,830
Mine are still under my chair.

302
00:18:07,980 --> 00:18:12,900
My stars are off duty. They know where to go
tomorrow.

303
00:18:15,250 --> 00:18:19,186
Did Grandma tease you about that tie for
years?

304
00:18:19,336 --> 00:18:22,414
Kindly. Then she helped me find one.

305
00:18:22,564 --> 00:18:25,214
A family tradition: notice, then help.

306
00:18:25,364 --> 00:18:29,300
I'm glad the family remembers the helping part
too.

307
00:18:30,250 --> 00:18:33,410
The label worked because we asked first.

308
00:18:33,560 --> 00:18:34,410
Exactly.

309
00:18:34,560 --> 00:18:38,160
A name cannot make the wrong pair yours.

310
00:18:38,310 --> 00:18:43,230
Good. My stars won't turn into dots behind a
different name.

311
00:18:45,250 --> 00:18:48,650
Top row for adults. Bottom row for us.

312
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:51,784
Mine is bottom-right when I face it.

313
00:18:51,934 --> 00:18:54,919
Directions with a starting point. Very
helpful.

314
00:18:55,069 --> 00:18:59,300
Exactly. Turn toward the shelf first and the
directions work.

315
00:19:00,250 --> 00:19:03,850
Another day, we can organize the garden pots.

316
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,720
Do plants need name labels too?

317
00:19:06,870 --> 00:19:09,150
A question for another afternoon.

318
00:19:09,300 --> 00:19:13,780
I wonder whether Dad will give the pots
apartments too.

319
00:19:15,250 --> 00:19:18,850
Grandma does not need a label, does she?

320
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,280
She introduced herself years ago.

321
00:19:21,430 --> 00:19:24,150
And remembers my packing history perfectly.

322
00:19:24,300 --> 00:19:28,780
Good. That story was worth bringing along in
her suitcase.

323
00:19:30,250 --> 00:19:33,850
Six pairs at home. One suitcase correctly
packed.

324
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,720
And one generous guest resting peacefully.

325
00:19:36,870 --> 00:19:39,150
That is a good afternoon.

326
00:19:39,300 --> 00:19:43,780
And she'll wake up to a room that's still
hers.

327
00:19:45,250 --> 00:19:49,730
Next time I help, I will ask where things
belong.

328
00:19:49,880 --> 00:19:52,600
That sounds like a wonderful welcome.

329
00:19:52,750 --> 00:19:54,150
Especially for socks.

330
00:19:54,300 --> 00:19:59,220
Even better if you ask before you close the
zipper, Dad.
